<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Application
    x:Class="Allodia.Mailcal.App"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <XamlControlsResources xmlns="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

            <!-- The caption every window of this app draws for itself (Services/WindowCaption.cs).
                 Here rather than in either of the two files that write one, MainWindow.xaml and
                 Views/WindowShell.xaml, because a height or an icon that differed between them would
                 read as two applications; the UI suite reads the height from this key too.

                 48 epx, which is what the TitleBar control takes when it carries content of its own.
                 None of ours does (the shell's search field is a sibling of it, MainWindow.xaml says
                 why), so each states 48 as a floor and the control centres its 32 in that. It is
                 also the height WindowCaption asks the system for its caption buttons, which is what
                 puts their glyphs on the same line as the icon and the name.

                 The PNG, not app.ico. WinUI decodes an .ico to its FIRST frame, which in ours is the
                 16px one (Images/generate-assets.ps1 writes them smallest-first, as the format
                 expects), and the caption then scales that up: 32 physical pixels of a 16-pixel
                 picture on a 200% display, which reads as a soft, smeared logo beside a crisp title.
                 A single-resolution source lets the decoder scale DOWN.

                 Neither of these is a brush, which is why they can live on the application at all: a
                 brush read from here answers with the theme the APPLICATION is in, and the app's
                 appearance is set on a content root instead (MainWindow.Theme.cs). -->
            <x:Double x:Key="AppCaptionHeight">48</x:Double>
            <ImageIconSource x:Key="AppCaptionIcon" ImageSource="ms-appx:///Images/TitleBarLogo.png" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
